-- title:
--   Ideal of the variables equals kernel of constantCoeff
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $n$ be a natural number, and consider the ring $R[[X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}]]$ of formal power series in the finitely many variables indexed by `Fin n`. The assertion is an equality of two ideals of this ring: the ideal generated by the range of the family of variables $X : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to R[[X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}]]$, that is, the ideal $(X_0,\dots,X_{n-1})$ spanned by the set $\{X_i : i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n\}$, coincides with the kernel of the ring homomorphism $\mathrm{constantCoeff} : R[[X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}]] \to R$ sending a power series to its coefficient at the zero exponent vector. In particular a power series lies in the ideal generated by the variables exactly when its constant term vanishes; no hypothesis beyond commutativity of $R$ is imposed, and the number of variables is finite, which is what makes the ideal-theoretic (rather than merely topological) equality correct. For $n = 0$ both sides are the zero ideal of $R$.
--
--   This is the standard identification of the augmentation ideal of a power series ring in finitely many variables over $R$ with the ideal of the variables, so that the quotient by $(X_0,\dots,X_{n-1})$ is $R$. It serves the presentation machinery for deformation rings, where $\mathcal{O}[[X_1,\dots,X_n]]$ is mapped onto a Hecke or deformation algebra and the augmentation $f \mapsto f(0)$ must be identified with reduction modulo the variables; it is used, among others, in the construction of isomorphisms between adic completions and quotients of power series rings, and in the study of invariants in formal module settings.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPowerSeries_span_range_X_eq_ker_constantCoeff.lean

import Mathlib.RingTheory.Regular.RegularSequence
import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Basic
import Mathlib.Data.Finsupp.Order

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

open MvPowerSeries

theorem MvPowerSeries.span_range_X_eq_ker_constantCoeff {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (n : ℕ) :
    Ideal.span (Set.range (X : Fin n → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) R)) =
      RingHom.ker (constantCoeff : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) R →+* R) := by sorry
